.TH PUTS 3S
.SH NAME
puts, fputs \- put a string on a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "#include <stdio.h>"
.sp
.B "puts(s)"
.B "char *s;"
.sp
.B "fputs(s, stream)"
.B "char *s;"
.B "FILE *stream;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B puts
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the standard output stream
.B stdout
and appends a newline character.
.PP
.B fputs
copies the null-terminated string
.I s
to the named output
.IR stream .
.PP
Neither routine copies the terminal null character.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
fopen(3), gets(3), putc(3), printf(3), ferror(3)
.br
fread(3) for
.B fwrite
.SH BUGS
.B puts
appends a newline,
.B fputs
does not, all in the name of backward
compatibility.
.sp
